      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_53aea4d1",
      "@type": "VisualMaterial",
      "instance_name": "four_klan_members",
      "label": "four individuals dressed in Ku Klux Klan robes",
      "description": "Four people standing side-by-side, wearing white hooded robes with pointed hoods and black circular emblems on the chest. Their faces are obscured by the hoods and masks. They appear to be posing for a photograph, likely from the early 20th century, given the black-and-white film quality and the biplane visible in the background. The robes are tied at the waist with ropes or cords. The visual material is historically significant and carries strong connotations of white supremacist violence.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_5afd843b",
      "@type": "Scene",
      "instance_name": "ku_klan_members_pose",
      "label": "Ku Klux Klan members posing for a photograph",
      "description": "Four Ku Klux Klan members are standing side-by-side in a row, dressed in white hooded robes with pointed hoods and black circular emblems on their chests. Their faces are obscured by masks. They appear to be posing for a photograph, likely from the early 20th century, with a biplane visible in the background. The scene is static and formal, suggesting a staged group portrait.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_8ef2befb",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "historical_ku_klan_violence",
      "label": "Historical Ku Klux Klan violence against Black Americans",
      "description": "The Ku Klux Klan was a white supremacist terrorist organization in the United States that engaged in violent acts, including lynchings, against Black people, particularly during the Reconstruction era and into the 20th century. The statistic of 3,446 Black people killed over 86 years refers to documented lynchings and other racially motivated killings attributed to the KKK and related white supremacist groups. This requires external historical knowledge to understand the context and scale of the violence depicted by the visual of the KKK members.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9b3563e1",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "contemporary_black_on_black_violence_in_chicago",
      "label": "Contemporary Black-on-Black violence in Chicago",
      "description": "The statistic claiming 1,891 Black people killed by other Black people in Chicago since 2016 refers to homicide data from the city. This requires external knowledge of urban crime statistics, racial demographics, and social issues in Chicago to understand the context and significance of the claim. The meme uses this statistic to create a false equivalence with historical KKK violence.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_8819002c",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "false_equivalence_racism",
      "label": "False equivalence between historical systemic racism and contemporary Black-on-Black violence",
      "description": "The meme's core argument relies on a false equivalence, implying that contemporary Black-on-Black violence is comparable to or even worse than historical white supremacist violence. This requires understanding of sociological and historical context: historical violence was systemic, institutional, and racially motivated by white supremacy, while contemporary violence is often the result of complex social, economic, and systemic issues within communities. The meme uses this false equivalence to deflect attention from systemic racism and to incite racial division.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_ca1a7d20",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "systemic_racism",
      "label": "Systemic racism and its historical impact on Black communities",
      "description": "The historical violence by the KKK was part of a broader system of racial oppression, including Jim Crow laws, disenfranchisement, and economic exploitation. Understanding this requires external knowledge of U.S. racial history and the concept of systemic racism, which is central to interpreting why the KKK's violence was so devastating and how it was sustained over decades.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_684a26c8",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "racial_division_and_hostility",
      "label": "Racial division and hostility as a social consequence of the meme",
      "description": "The meme's intent is to provoke racial division and hostility by framing contemporary Black-on-Black violence as a 'problem' that must be addressed internally, while minimizing the historical and systemic nature of white supremacist violence. This requires understanding of social dynamics, racial tensions, and how such memes can be used to incite prejudice and fear.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_177012a3",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "white_supremacist_terrorism",
      "label": "White supremacist terrorism and its historical context",
      "description": "The Ku Klux Klan is a historical example of white supremacist terrorism. Understanding the nature of this terrorism — its use of violence to maintain racial hierarchy, its targeting of Black communities, and its role in suppressing civil rights — requires external knowledge of U.S. history and the concept of terrorism as a tool of racial oppression.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_97216dbb",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "urban_crime_statistics",
      "label": "Urban crime statistics and racial disparities in homicide rates",
      "description": "The statistic about 1,891 Black people killed by other Black people in Chicago since 2016 requires external knowledge of urban crime data, racial disparities in homicide rates, and the social factors contributing to violence in urban communities. This knowledge is necessary to understand the context and validity of the claim.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_77698b59",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "historical_context_of_lynching",
      "label": "Historical context of lynching in the United States",
      "description": "The statistic of 3,446 Black people killed by the KKK over 86 years refers to lynching, a form of extrajudicial punishment and racial terror. Understanding the historical context of lynching — its prevalence, its purpose, and its impact on Black communities — requires external knowledge of U.S. racial history and the history of racial violence.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_bdae0928"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_e7f71c89",
